[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Lambda Expressions: Syntax, Functional Interfaces"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Lambda Expressions"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lambda expressions, introduced in Java 8, provide a concise way to represent anonymous functions. They enable you to treat functionality as a method argument, or code as data. Lambda expressions are particularly useful when working with functional interfaces."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Syntax of Lambda Expressions"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The general syntax of a lambda expression is as follows:"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"(parameters) -> expression

(parameters) -> { statements; }"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parameters: The input parameters to the lambda expression. Can be zero or more."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"->: The arrow operator, separating the parameters from the lambda body."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Expression: A single expression that the lambda evaluates and returns. Can be a simple calculation or a method call."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Statements: A block of code enclosed in curly braces {}. Used when the lambda body needs to perform multiple operations.  If using statements, you must explicitly use the `return` keyword if the lambda is expected to return a value."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Examples of Lambda Expressions"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here are some examples demonstrating different lambda expression syntaxes:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// No parameters, returns a String
() -> "Hello World"

// Single parameter, returns the parameter squared
n -> n * n

// Two parameters, returns their sum
(a, b) -> a + b

// Two parameters with explicit types, returns their concatenation
(String firstName, String lastName) -> firstName + " " + lastName

// Two parameters, block of code, returns their product
(int x, int y) -> {
    int product = x * y;
    return product;
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Functional Interfaces"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"A functional interface is an interface that contains only one abstract method (Single Abstract Method or SAM). It can have multiple default or static methods. Functional interfaces provide the target type for lambda expressions."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java provides several built-in functional interfaces in the `java.util.function` package, such as `Predicate`, `Consumer`, `Function`, and `Supplier`. You can also define your own functional interfaces."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Built-in Functional Interfaces"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Predicate<T>`: Represents a predicate (boolean-valued function) of one argument.  It has a method `test(T t)` which returns a boolean."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Consumer<T>`: Represents an operation that accepts a single input argument and returns no result. It has a method `accept(T t)`."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Function<T, R>`: Represents a function that accepts one argument and produces a result. It has a method `apply(T t)` which returns an `R`."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Supplier<T>`: Represents a supplier of results. It has a method `get()` which returns a `T`."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`UnaryOperator<T>`: Represents an operation on a single operand that produces a result of the same type as its operand. Extends `Function<T, T>`."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`BinaryOperator<T>`: Represents an operation upon two operands of the same type, producing a result of the same type as the operands. Extends `Function<T, T, T>`."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Built-in Functional Interfaces with Lambda Expressions"},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.function.Predicate;
import java.util.function.Consumer;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

public class FunctionalInterfaceExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Predicate: Check if a number is even
        Predicate<Integer> isEven = n -> n % 2 == 0;
        System.out.println("Is 4 even? " + isEven.test(4)); // Output: true

        // Consumer: Print a message
        Consumer<String> greet = message -> System.out.println("Hello, " + message);
        greet.accept("World"); // Output: Hello, World

        // Function: Convert a string to uppercase
        Function<String, String> toUpperCase = str -> str.toUpperCase();
        System.out.println(toUpperCase.apply("java")); // Output: JAVA

        // Supplier: Generate a random number
        Supplier<Double> randomNumber = () -> Math.random();
        System.out.println("Random number: " + randomNumber.get());
    }
}"},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Your Own Functional Interface"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define your own functional interface using the `@FunctionalInterface` annotation. This annotation is optional but recommended, as it instructs the compiler to enforce the single abstract method rule."},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"@FunctionalInterface
interface StringConverter {
    String convert(String text);
}

public class CustomFunctionalInterfaceExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Using the custom functional interface with a lambda expression
        StringConverter toLower = text -> text.toLowerCase();
        System.out.println(toLower.convert("JAVA")); // Output: java
    }
}"},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Method References"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"Method references are a shorthand notation for lambda expressions that call an existing method. They can make your code more readable."},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"There are four kinds of method references:"},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to a static method: `ClassName::staticMethodName`"},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to an instance method of a particular object: `object::instanceMethodName`"},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to an instance method of an arbitrary object of a particular type: `ClassName::instanceMethodName`"},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to a constructor: `ClassName::new`"},{"order":36,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Function;

public class MethodReferenceExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");

        // Method reference to a static method
        names.forEach(System.out::println);

        // Method reference to an instance method of a particular object
        String prefix = "Name: ";
        names.forEach(name -> System.out.println(prefix + name)); //Lambda Expression
        names.forEach(new ConsumerWithPrefix(prefix)::printName); //Method Reference

        // Method reference to an instance method of an arbitrary object of a particular type
        Function<String, String> toUpperCase = String::toUpperCase;
        System.out.println(toUpperCase.apply("java"));

    }
}

class ConsumerWithPrefix {
    private String prefix;

    public ConsumerWithPrefix(String prefix) {
        this.prefix = prefix;
    }

    public void printName(String name) {
        System.out.println(this.prefix + name);
    }
}"},{"order":37,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lambda expressions and functional interfaces are powerful features in Java that promote functional programming paradigms. They allow you to write more concise and readable code, especially when working with collections and streams. Understanding their syntax and usage is crucial for modern Java development."}]
